    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Four hundred people once danced continuously until they dropped dead. In 1518, a woman stepped into a French street and started moving uncontrollably.",
        "visual_keywords": "old cobblestone street fog"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Within weeks, hundreds joined her. They couldn't stop, weeping in pain as their bloody feet kept stomping.",
        "visual_keywords": "shadowy crowd silhouette moving"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Authorities even brought in musicians to help, but it only made the obsession deadlier. What was possessing them?",
        "visual_keywords": "musicians playing dark room"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Modern historians finally solved it: severe famine triggered mass psychogenic illness. Extreme psychological stress forced their nervous systems into involuntary spasms.",
        "visual_keywords": "old medical manuscript reading"
      },
      {
        "narration": "This outbreak proved human minds under terror can physically drive bodies to death.",
        "visual_keywords": "empty dark town square"
      }
    ],
